{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"4fa2dbb9ddda3356","name":"card_event_shifting_attack","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/CovertOp/events/card_event_shifting_attack","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SHIFTING ATTACK","text":"Send a friendly unit to hand. It costs 0 kredits to deploy this turn.","flavorText":"As the war progressed, Germany would often redeploy units to new fronts and weaken existing ones. This was especially true in anticipation of the Allied landings in France. In early 1944 the German Western Front was significantly weakened by personnel and materiel being transferred to the Eastern Front. For instance, the entire II SS Panzer Corps was relocated from France to the Eastern Front to face the Soviet Dnieper-Carpathian Offensive. This included two elite SS Panzer Divisions (the 9th and 10th), an infantry division, a heavy tank battalion, and two assault gun brigades. Overall, this removed nearly 46,000 soldiers and over 360 armored vehicles from France, which severely impacted Germans defensive capabilities on the Western Front. This was a major change in German strategy, as previous orders had restricted such large transfers from the west to the east.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_shifting_attack","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_shifting_attack_title","SourceString":"SHIFTING ATTACK","LocalizedString":"SHIFTING ATTACK"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_shifting_attack_text","SourceString":"Send a friendly unit to hand. It costs 0 kredits to deploy this turn.","LocalizedString":"Send a friendly unit to hand. It costs 0 kredits to deploy this turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_shifting_attack_history","SourceString":"As the war progressed, Germany would often redeploy units to new fronts and weaken existing ones. This was especially true in anticipation of the Allied landings in France. In early 1944 the German Western Front was significantly weakened by personnel and materiel being transferred to the Eastern Front. For instance, the entire II SS Panzer Corps was relocated from France to the Eastern Front to face the Soviet Dnieper-Carpathian Offensive. This included two elite SS Panzer Divisions (the 9th and 10th), an infantry division, a heavy tank battalion, and two assault gun brigades. Overall, this removed nearly 46,000 soldiers and over 360 armored vehicles from France, which severely impacted Germans defensive capabilities on the Western Front. This was a major change in German strategy, as previous orders had restricted such large transfers from the west to the east.","LocalizedString":"As the war progressed, Germany would often redeploy units to new fronts and weaken existing ones. This was especially true in anticipation of the Allied landings in France. In early 1944 the German Western Front was significantly weakened by personnel and materiel being transferred to the Eastern Front. For instance, the entire II SS Panzer Corps was relocated from France to the Eastern Front to face the Soviet Dnieper-Carpathian Offensive. This included two elite SS Panzer Divisions (the 9th and 10th), an infantry division, a heavy tank battalion, and two assault gun brigades. Overall, this removed nearly 46,000 soldiers and over 360 armored vehicles from France, which severely impacted Germans defensive capabilities on the Western Front. This was a major change in German strategy, as previous orders had restricted such large transfers from the west to the east."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::CurrentExpansion","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardReset"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Eagl